    {
        'title': 'TOEFL Reading · Academic Passage 45: Hospital Antibiotic Stewardship Programs',
        'content': "# Read an Academic Passage — Hospital Antibiotic Stewardship Programs\n\n**Discipline:** Medicine  \n**Task type:** TOEFL iBT Reading · Read an Academic Passage  \n**Word count:** ~532 words\n\n---\n\nAntibiotics transformed infectious disease treatment, yet their overuse selects for resistant bacteria that no longer respond to standard drugs. Hospitals concentrate both vulnerable patients and intensive antimicrobial use, making them critical sites for stewardship: organized efforts to ensure that the right drug, dose, and duration are chosen, and that antibiotics are avoided when they will not help. Stewardship programs typically include infectious-disease physicians, pharmacists, microbiologists, and infection-prevention nurses who review prescriptions, issue guidelines, and educate staff.\n\nEvidence shows that targeted interventions can reduce unnecessary broad-spectrum use without increasing mortality when alternative treatments are appropriate. Rapid diagnostic tests that distinguish bacterial from viral infections help clinicians avoid antibiotics for conditions such as many respiratory illnesses. Time-out procedures, in which teams reassess therapy after forty-eight hours when culture results return, shorten courses that were started empirically. Formulary restrictions on last-line agents prevent casual use of drugs needed for resistant infections. These measures require reliable laboratory capacity; without timely cultures, physicians may continue wide-spectrum therapy out of caution.\n\nResistance is not only a hospital problem. Community prescribing, agricultural use, and global travel move resistant genes across borders. Still, hospital data are often the most complete, so they dominate surveillance reports. Metrics such as days of therapy per thousand patient-days allow comparison across wards, but they can be gamed if hospitals shift patients or coding practices. Outcome measures—Clostridioides difficile rates, resistance trends, and length of stay—matter more to patients than prescription counts alone. Qualitative studies reveal that junior doctors may fear criticism for withholding antibiotics more than for overprescribing, a cultural barrier that guidelines alone do not remove.\n\nEthical tensions arise when stewardship delays a preferred drug for a severely ill patient, or when cost-saving is perceived as the hidden motive. Transparent criteria and rapid appeal pathways help maintain trust. In low-resource hospitals, the challenge may be access rather than excess: stockouts force switches that undermine protocols. International collaborations therefore frame stewardship as both conservation of existing drugs and investment in diagnostics, sanitation, and vaccination that reduce infection demand.\n\nReaders of medical social-science and clinical review articles should notice how authors balance individual care with collective risk. A patient who receives an unnecessary antibiotic may recover equally well, while the population faces a slightly higher chance of future untreatable infection. That mismatch of timescales makes stewardship a classic public-goods problem inside clinical medicine. Test questions may ask for the primary purpose of stewardship, which is optimizing use rather than eliminating antibiotics. Hedge language appears when discussing how much resistance can be reversed even if prescribing improves, because already circulating genes do not disappear quickly. The most careful passages also mention that prevention—hand hygiene, device care, and vaccination—reduces the need to prescribe in the first place, situating drugs within a broader infection-control system rather than as a standalone technological fix.\n\nMedical students reading stewardship literature should also notice the role of diagnostic uncertainty at night, when senior consultants are fewer and cultures have not returned. Protocols that work at noon may fail at 2:00 a.m. unless they include backup advice lines. Understanding this organizational context prevents a naive conclusion that resistance is only a matter of individual doctors ignoring science. Systems design is part of clinical evidence.\n\n---\n\n*Study tip:* Summarize each paragraph in one sentence, then state the author's overall purpose in a single line.*\n",
        'category': 'TOEFL Reading',
        'space': 'toefl-reading',
    },
    {
        'title': 'TOEFL Reading · Academic Passage 46: Urban Heat Islands and Neighborhood Inequality',
        'content': "# Read an Academic Passage — Urban Heat Islands and Neighborhood Inequality\n\n**Discipline:** Geography  \n**Task type:** TOEFL iBT Reading · Read an Academic Passage  \n**Word count:** ~526 words\n\n---\n\nCities are often warmer than surrounding countryside, a pattern called the urban heat island. Buildings, dark pavements, and reduced vegetation store and reradiate heat, while waste heat from vehicles and air conditioners adds to the load. The effect is strongest at night, when rural areas cool faster than dense neighborhoods. Heat islands are not uniform: satellite and weather-station data show that some census tracts can be several degrees hotter than others in the same metro area. Those differences frequently align with historical housing discrimination, tree-canopy loss, and industrial land uses.\n\nPublic-health researchers link extreme heat to hospitalizations, reduced outdoor work capacity, and excess deaths, especially among older adults, outdoor laborers, and people without air conditioning. During heat waves, the urban increment can push already dangerous temperatures into lethal ranges. Cooling centers, warning systems, and utility policies that prevent shutoffs are short-term responses. Longer-term strategies include reflective roofs, lighter pavements, street trees, and parks that provide shade and evapotranspiration. Each intervention has limits. Reflective surfaces may increase glare or winter heating needs in some climates. Trees require water, maintenance, and space that compete with parking and utilities. Parks can raise nearby housing costs if greening is not paired with anti-displacement policies.\n\nMeasurement choices affect which neighborhoods appear most at risk. Land-surface temperature from satellites describes roof and pavement heat, whereas air temperature at pedestrian height better matches human exposure. Humidity, wind, and access to indoor cooling further modify risk, so maps based on temperature alone can mislead. Community science projects that distribute sensors help fill gaps between official weather stations, which are often sited at airports rather than in dense housing.\n\nGovernance is fragmented. Transportation departments control streets, parks departments manage trees, housing agencies influence building codes, and energy utilities shape air-conditioning demand. Coordinated heat plans are still uncommon. Some cities now require cool roofs on new construction or target tree planting in the hottest tracts. Evaluation remains essential because a planted sapling does not equal mature canopy for decades. For geography students, heat islands connect physical climatology to environmental justice: the same atmospheric process produces unequal harm depending on urban form and political history. Academic passages often present a physical mechanism first, then social distribution, then policy options. Readers should track whether claimed temperature gaps refer to surface or air measurements and whether health outcomes are observed or modeled. Authors may cite redlining maps overlaid with modern canopy data to argue that past policy still structures present risk. That historical claim is distinct from the physical claim that pavement stores heat; both can be true, and test items sometimes ask which paragraph supports which type of evidence. Understanding scale—from a single block’s asphalt to a regional climate trend—prevents overgeneralizing one neighborhood’s experience to all cities.\n\nClimate change raises baseline temperatures so that yesterday’s rare heat island peak becomes a more frequent event. Adaptation planning therefore cannot treat historical weather as a stable design standard. Planners who combine shade, cooling access, and emergency outreach will do better than those who install a single technology. Readers should look for whether a passage discusses compound hazards such as heat plus power outages, which often drive the worst health outcomes.\n\n---\n\n*Study tip:* Summarize each paragraph in one sentence, then state the author's overall purpose in a single line.*\n",
        'category': 'TOEFL Reading',
        'space': 'toefl-reading',
    },
    {
        'title': 'TOEFL Reading · Academic Passage 47: Sign Languages as Full Linguistic Systems',
        'content': "# Read an Academic Passage — Sign Languages as Full Linguistic Systems\n\n**Discipline:** Linguistics  \n**Task type:** TOEFL iBT Reading · Read an Academic Passage  \n**Word count:** ~543 words\n\n---\n\nSign languages used by Deaf communities are not pantomime or broken versions of spoken languages. They have phonology-like structure in handshape, location, movement, and facial grammar; they have morphology that builds complex words; and they have syntax that orders constituents according to language-specific rules. Linguists document hundreds of sign languages, many of them unrelated to the surrounding spoken language. American Sign Language, for example, is historically connected to French Sign Language more closely than to English. This fact surprises students who assume that signs simply translate local speech word for word.\n\nResearch on child language acquisition shows that Deaf children exposed to a rich sign language from infancy follow developmental milestones comparable to hearing children learning speech. When Deaf children are denied accessible language, whether signed or spoken, they may experience delays that are social rather than biological in origin. Homesign systems invented by isolated Deaf children demonstrate impressive creativity but typically lack the full conventional grammar of a community language. Studies of emerging sign languages, such as those documented in some schools and villages, reveal how grammar can conventionalize over cohorts, offering a rare window into language formation.\n\nNeuroscience finds that sign languages activate classic left-hemisphere language networks, even though the articulators are the hands and face rather than the vocal tract. This evidence undermines the idea that language is inherently spoken. At the same time, sign languages exploit spatial grammar in ways speech cannot, using locations in signing space to track referents. Interpreters and bilingual Deaf people navigate two modalities, and code-blending research examines how they mix features under different social conditions.\n\nEducational and civil-rights debates follow from the linguistic status of sign. If sign languages are complete languages, then access to them is a language right, not merely a disability accommodation. Captioning, interpreters, and bilingual education models become questions of equity. Opponents of early sign exposure once claimed that signing would prevent spoken-language development; empirical work generally finds that accessible language, including sign, supports later literacy and additional language learning rather than blocking it. Variation within sign languages—regional dialects, racialized signing styles, and age-graded change—further shows that they behave like other living languages.\n\nAcademic reading on this topic often corrects misconceptions in the first paragraphs, then presents structural evidence, then discusses social consequences. TOEFL-style questions may ask why the author mentions French Sign Language (to show historical relationships independent of English) or what homesign lacks relative to community sign languages. Readers should distinguish modality (visual-manual versus auditory-vocal) from linguistic complexity. Hedge language appears around counts of sign languages worldwide because documentation is incomplete and some varieties sit on a dialect-language boundary. The passage’s broader lesson is that human language is a cognitive and social capacity that can be realized in more than one physical channel, a claim with implications for education, neuroscience, and human-rights law.\n\nInterpreter education and technology raise additional questions: video relay services expand access, yet they also introduce lag and camera-angle problems that can omit grammatical face movement. Linguists studying sign in digital environments therefore treat the medium as part of the grammar’s ecology. Students should remember that a language can be complete even when a particular video call delivers it poorly, just as a phone line can mangle speech without proving that spoken language is deficient.\n\n---\n\n*Study tip:* Summarize each paragraph in one sentence, then state the author's overall purpose in a single line.*\n",
        'category': 'TOEFL Reading',
        'space': 'toefl-reading',
    },
    {
        'title': 'TOEFL Reading · Academic Passage 48: Lithium Battery Recycling and Supply Chains',
        'content': "# Read an Academic Passage — Lithium Battery Recycling and Supply Chains\n\n**Discipline:** Environmental Science  \n**Task type:** TOEFL iBT Reading · Read an Academic Passage  \n**Word count:** ~526 words\n\n---\n\nRechargeable lithium-ion batteries power phones, laptops, and a growing fleet of electric vehicles. Mining lithium, cobalt, nickel, and graphite has environmental and social costs, including water use in arid regions, habitat disruption, and labor concerns in some cobalt-producing areas. Recycling spent batteries could recover metals, reduce pressure on mines, and keep hazardous materials out of ordinary landfills. Yet collection rates remain low for consumer electronics, and automotive packs are only now reaching end of life in large numbers. The gap between recycling’s promise and current practice is a frequent subject of environmental-science reviews.\n\nTechnical routes include pyrometallurgy, which uses high heat to recover some metals; hydrometallurgy, which uses chemical leaching; and direct recycling, which aims to refurbish cathode materials without fully breaking them down. Each method trades energy use, recovery rates, and purity. Battery designs that glue cells into packs or mix chemistries complicate disassembly. Manufacturers have begun to experiment with labels, modular packs, and design-for-recycling standards, but products already sold will dominate waste streams for years. Safety is a practical barrier: damaged cells can ignite, so transport and storage require trained handlers.\n\nEconomics depends on metal prices. When newly mined nickel or cobalt is cheap, recyclers may struggle to compete unless policy requires recycled content or imposes disposal fees. Some jurisdictions now mandate producer responsibility, shifting collection costs onto brands. Informal recycling in places without worker protections can recover value while exposing people to toxins, so formalization and technology transfer are part of the governance problem, not only a matter of building plants in wealthy countries.\n\nLife-cycle analyses caution against treating recycling as a complete solution. Manufacturing still requires energy; collection logistics emit carbon; and not every element is recovered at high yield. Reducing pack size through efficiency, extending vehicle life with repair, and second-use applications—such as using retired automotive batteries for stationary storage—can delay recycling while extracting more service from the same materials. These strategies interact: a second-use market may reduce the volume available to recyclers in the short term while still lowering overall mining demand.\n\nStudents reading this literature should separate three questions: Can metals be recovered technically? Will they be recovered at scale under current prices? And does recycling address mining harms in producer regions, or merely relocate demand? Authors often present a flowchart from mine to vehicle to waste, then identify bottlenecks at collection rather than at chemistry. Vocabulary such as cathode, feedstock, and closed loop appears with definitions nearby. Inference items may ask why low collection rates matter even if laboratory recycling efficiency is high. The most careful texts also note that battery chemistries are changing; a plant optimized for one cathode type may be less efficient for the next generation. Environmental assessment, therefore, must be updated as products evolve rather than treated as a one-time calculation.\n\nTrade policy and mineral diplomacy further influence whether recycling plants are built near manufacturing or near waste. If cathode production stays far from collection, transport emissions rise and economic cases weaken. Passages that mention ‘circular economy’ should be checked for actual reverse-logistics detail. Without collection, labeling, and skilled disassembly, circularity remains a slogan. That operational emphasis is what distinguishes environmental-science writing from marketing.\n\n---\n\n*Study tip:* Summarize each paragraph in one sentence, then state the author's overall purpose in a single line.*\n",
        'category': 'TOEFL Reading',
        'space': 'toefl-reading',
    },
    {
        'title': 'TOEFL Reading · Academic Passage 49: Memory Reconsolidation and Clinical Translation',
        'content': "# Read an Academic Passage — Memory Reconsolidation and Clinical Translation\n\n**Discipline:** Psychology  \n**Task type:** TOEFL iBT Reading · Read an Academic Passage  \n**Word count:** ~520 words\n\n---\n\nFor much of the twentieth century, psychologists treated long-term memories as relatively stable once consolidated. Newer research on reconsolidation proposes that when a memory is reactivated, it can temporarily return to a labile state in which it may be updated or weakened before being stored again. Animal studies using fear conditioning found that drugs or extinction training delivered during this window can reduce later fear responses. The finding generated excitement about treating post-traumatic stress, phobias, and addiction by modifying troublesome memories rather than merely teaching people to suppress them.\n\nHuman translation has been mixed. Some laboratory studies report that presenting a reminder, then delivering extinction training or a pharmacological agent, reduces emotional responses more than extinction alone. Other experiments fail to replicate the effect, or find it only under narrow timing, reminder, and intensity conditions. Memories that are old, strongly rehearsed, or embedded in complex narratives may not behave like a single conditioned freeze response in a rodent. Ethical constraints also limit the drugs researchers can administer to patients. As a result, clinical protocols remain experimental, and professional guidelines do not treat reconsolidation blockade as standard care.\n\nConceptual debates continue. Skeptics argue that apparent reconsolidation effects may be ordinary extinction, interference, or amnesia for the reminder context rather than rewriting of the original trace. Distinguishing those mechanisms requires clever experimental designs, such as testing whether fear returns in a new context. Neuroimaging studies attempt to show updated representations, but imaging correlates are not the same as proof of molecular reconsolidation in humans. Terminology in popular media often overstates the idea of “erasing” memory, which alarms patients and misrepresents the science. Researchers emphasize weakening of emotional intensity or updating of predictions, not deletion of autobiographical facts.\n\nDespite uncertainty, the reconsolidation framework has influenced therapy research by focusing attention on timing: when during a session a traumatic memory is activated, how long clinicians wait, and what new information is provided. Even if the molecular story is incomplete, process research on memory activation may improve exposure therapies. For psychology students, the topic models how a precise laboratory phenomenon can generate clinical hope, followed by a period of boundary testing. Academic passages typically narrate the animal discovery, then human attempts, then caveats. Reading questions often target why replication failures matter or why timing is theoretically important. Hedge verbs—suggest, may, under certain conditions—are abundant and should not be flattened into certainty. Students should also notice the difference between fear memories created in the lab and trauma memories formed in life, a distinction authors use to limit generalization. The broader lesson is that memory is active and reconstructive, a theme that reconsolidation research shares with older work on eyewitness testimony, even when the proposed clinical tools remain provisional.\n\nTraining clinics sometimes over-adopt laboratory procedures before boundary conditions are known, a pattern psychology has seen with other fashionable interventions. Responsible authors therefore call for preregistered clinical trials with active control groups, not only case series. Readers should treat reconsolidation not as a guaranteed key to trauma therapy but as a hypothesis that sharpened questions about timing and memory activation. Intellectual caution is part of scientific literacy here.\n\n---\n\n*Study tip:* Summarize each paragraph in one sentence, then state the author's overall purpose in a single line.*\n",
        'category': 'TOEFL Reading',
        'space': 'toefl-reading',
    },
    {
        'title': 'TOEFL Reading · Academic Passage 50: Indigenous Fire Stewardship and Landscape Ecology',
        'content': "# Read an Academic Passage — Indigenous Fire Stewardship and Landscape Ecology\n\n**Discipline:** Ecology  \n**Task type:** TOEFL iBT Reading · Read an Academic Passage  \n**Word count:** ~524 words\n\n---\n\nMany ecosystems evolved with fire, yet twentieth-century suppression policies in several countries aimed to extinguish wildfires quickly. Fuel accumulated, and when ignitions occurred under extreme weather, fires became larger and harder to control. Parallel to this history, Indigenous nations in Australia, North America, and elsewhere maintained burning practices that created mosaics of vegetation ages, reduced understory density, and protected culturally important plants and settlements. Landscape ecologists and fire scientists have increasingly documented that these practices are not merely cultural heritage but ecological knowledge with measurable effects on biodiversity and hazard.\n\nStudies of cultural burning describe cool-season, low-intensity fires set under specific wind and moisture conditions, often in small patches. Such fires can limit the continuity of fuels that carry crown fires, stimulate germination of fire-adapted species, and maintain open woodlands used for hunting or gathering. Where Indigenous fire was interrupted by colonization, displacement, and legal bans, vegetation structure changed in ways that later complicated wildfire management. Restoring stewardship is not a simple return to a static past: climate change, invasive grasses, and fragmented land tenure create conditions unlike those of previous centuries. Partnerships that combine Indigenous leadership with atmospheric monitoring and aerial ignition can adapt practice rather than freeze it.\n\nConflicts arise over liability, smoke, and wildlife. Urban residents may oppose prescribed fire because of air quality, even when the alternative is a more severe wildfire later. Agencies trained in suppression may lack staffing calendars that match cultural burning seasons. Legal recognition of Indigenous jurisdiction over fire varies widely. Successful programs tend to invest in long-term relationships, training exchanges, and shared decision-making rather than extracting a technique from its social context.\n\nAcademic writing on this subject often challenges the myth of “wilderness” as land untouched by people. If many landscapes were shaped by purposeful fire, then conservation baselines that ignore human stewardship misread ecology. At the same time, authors caution against romanticizing all burning as automatically beneficial; poorly timed fire can damage soils, peat, or sensitive species. Evidence quality varies from experimental plots to oral history to charcoal records in lakes. Readers should note which claims rest on which sources.\n\nFor ecology students, Indigenous fire stewardship links disturbance ecology to environmental justice. Test questions may ask for the author’s attitude toward suppression policy or for a contrast between high-intensity wildfire and cultural burning. Vocabulary such as fuel load, mosaic, and prescribed fire is usually defined in context. Inference items might target why climate change complicates restoration of historical fire regimes. The most careful passages present Indigenous knowledge as dynamic expertise, not as a frozen tradition, and present Western fire science as one knowledge system among others rather than as the sole authority. That framing prepares readers to evaluate collaboration models on both ecological outcomes and governance fairness.\n\nInsurance and air-quality regulations can unintentionally block beneficial fire even when ecology supports it. Reform may require smoke-management plans and public education that distinguish a planned cultural burn from an uncontrolled wildfire. Students should notice when a passage treats law and ecology as coupled systems. A technically ideal burn that cannot be permitted will not occur, so governance evidence belongs in the same argument as flame-length statistics.\n\n---\n\n*Study tip:* Summarize each paragraph in one sentence, then state the author's overall purpose in a single line.*\n",
        'category': 'TOEFL Reading',
        'space': 'toefl-reading',
    },
